select energy_manager.load_cards($cards$[
{"id":"p3-b06-023","paper":3,"topic":"Flow Control","kind":"fact","front":"Why is bypass control less energy efficient than a control valve, and what is the small bypass line for?","back":"With bypass control the pump runs at maximum demand all the time, so its power does not fall with demand. A small bypass line installed to stop the pump running at zero flow is a safety feature, not a means of flow control.","explain":"A fixed-duty pump feeding a tank that overflows back to suction shows the same waste. The energy lost in bypass lines only shows up when the whole system is analysed.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p218","order":3323},
{"id":"p3-b06-024","paper":3,"topic":"Impeller Trimming","kind":"fact","front":"Why is impeller trimming used instead of buying a smaller impeller?","back":"The next smaller impeller is often too small for the load, and smaller impellers may not be made for that pump size. Trimming is then the only practical option short of replacing the pump and motor.","explain":"Trimming lowers the energy given to the liquid, removing the excess head. The affinity relations are non-linear for trimmed impellers, so predictions are approximate.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p218","order":3324},
{"id":"p3-b06-025","paper":3,"topic":"Impeller Trimming","kind":"problem","front":"A pump with a 300 mm impeller delivers 120 m³/h at 40 m head and 20 kW. The impeller is trimmed to 240 mm. What are the new flow, head and power?","back":"Flow 96 m³/h, head 25.6 m, power 10.2 kW.","explain":"Flow is proportional to diameter, head to diameter squared and power to diameter cubed. A 20% diameter cut reduces power by almost half. This is within the chapter's usual limit of trimming to about 75% of the original diameter.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p219","steps":[{"prompt":"Diameter ratio D2/D1 and whether it is within the usual trim limit?","answer":"240 / 300 = 0.80, which is above 0.75, so acceptable.","explain":"The chapter says impeller diameters are rarely reduced below 75% of original."},{"prompt":"New flow?","answer":"Q2 = 120 × 0.80 = 96 m³/h","explain":"Q2/Q1 = D2/D1."},{"prompt":"New head?","answer":"H2 = 40 × 0.80² = 25.6 m","explain":"H2/H1 = (D2/D1)²."},{"prompt":"New power?","answer":"P2 = 20 × 0.80³ = 10.2 kW","explain":"P2/P1 = (D2/D1)³."}],"order":3325},
{"id":"p3-b06-026","paper":3,"topic":"Variable Speed Drives","kind":"fact","front":"How do multiple-speed motors differ from variable speed drives (VSDs)?","back":"Multiple-speed motors have separate windings for each speed, cost more and are less efficient than single-speed motors, and offer only discrete speeds. VSDs give continuous speed adjustment without jumping between speeds.","explain":"The two serve separate applications, so the choice depends on whether the load needs fine or only step-wise speed changes.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p219","order":3326},
{"id":"p3-b06-027","paper":3,"topic":"Variable Speed Drives","kind":"fact","front":"Name the mechanical and electrical types of variable speed drive.","back":"Mechanical: hydraulic clutches, fluid couplings, adjustable belts and pulleys. Electrical: eddy current clutches, wound-rotor motor controllers and variable frequency drives (VFDs).","explain":"VFDs change the supply frequency to change motor speed. They are by far the most popular type of VSD.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p220","order":3327},
{"id":"p3-b06-028","paper":3,"topic":"Variable Speed Drives","kind":"fact","front":"What soft-start benefit does a VFD give a motor?","back":"A VFD cuts the start-up current from about 5 to 6 times normal operating current to about 1.5 times, which reduces wear on the motor and its controller.","explain":"A direct start draws a large in-rush current that fades as the motor reaches speed. The VFD ramps the speed up, so the starting current stays low.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p220","order":3328},
{"id":"p3-b06-029","paper":3,"topic":"Boiler Feed Water Pumps","kind":"fact","front":"Why do boiler feed water pumps often waste energy, and what fixes are possible?","back":"They are sized for design pressure, but most boilers run below it, so excess head is dropped across pressure-reducing and manual valves. Fixes: a VFD, trimming the impeller, reducing the number of stages, or changing the pump.","explain":"A VFD can cut pump power and improve control. The best option depends on how much the boiler load varies.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p221","order":3329},
{"id":"p3-b06-030","paper":3,"topic":"Boiler Feed Water Pumps","kind":"fact","front":"Name the three ways a VFD can control a boiler feed pump.","back":"1) One pump, one boiler, no feed water regulating valve: pump speed follows boiler water level. 2) Constant discharge pressure at a set value regardless of plant load. 3) Constant differential pressure of about 3.5 to 5.5 kg/cm² across the feed regulating valve.","explain":"Removing the regulating valve removes its throttling loss and some capital cost. Constant discharge pressure keeps the pump away from shut-off pressures.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p221","order":3330},
{"id":"p3-b06-031","paper":3,"topic":"Boiler Feed Water Pumps","kind":"problem","front":"A boiler feed pump draws 53 kW with 6 stages, and 35 kW after two impellers are removed (4 stages). What is the saving in kW and as a percentage?","back":"18 kW, about 34% of the original power.","explain":"Removing stages lowers the pressure the pump develops, so less head is thrown away across the throttling valves. The chapter reports these measured figures from an actual case.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p221","steps":[{"prompt":"Power saving in kW?","answer":"53 − 35 = 18 kW","explain":"Subtract the power after the change from the power before."},{"prompt":"Saving as a percentage of the original power?","answer":"18 / 53 = 0.34, so about 34%","explain":"Divide the saving by the original power."},{"prompt":"Why does removing stages save energy?","answer":"Less head is developed, so less pressure is throttled away at the valves.","explain":"The boiler runs below design pressure, so the extra stages were generating unused head."}],"order":3331},
{"id":"p3-b06-032","paper":3,"topic":"Municipal Water Pumping","kind":"fact","front":"What are the sub-systems of a municipal water pumping system?","back":"Raw water pump house at the intake, pure water pump house with filtration plant, booster stations as required, and elevated storage reservoirs in the distribution system.","explain":"A water pumping station's capacity is normally stated in million litres per day (MLD).","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p222","order":3332},
{"id":"p3-b06-033","paper":3,"topic":"Vertical Turbine Pumps","kind":"fact","front":"When is a vertical turbine pump used, and what are its drawbacks? What is a submersible pump?","back":"It is used where the pumping water level is below the limit of a volute centrifugal pump. Drawbacks: higher initial cost and harder to install and repair. A submersible pump is a vertical turbine pump close-coupled to a small submersible motor fixed below the intake, used in very deep tube wells.","explain":"A single impeller gives limited head, so more head is obtained by adding bowl assemblies (stages). A long drive shaft would be impractical in very deep wells, which is why the motor is submerged.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p222","order":3333},
{"id":"p3-b06-034","paper":3,"topic":"Sewage Pumps","kind":"fact","front":"How do sewage pump impellers differ from clear-water pump impellers, and why do sewage pumps use volutes?","back":"Sewage impellers are designed to pass solids that clog clear-water pumps. They are single-suction (no shaft in the intake) and radial-flow. They use volutes because a diffuser is prone to clogging.","explain":"Solids and fibrous matter would block diffuser passages, so the open volute is preferred for sewage handling.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p222","order":3334},
{"id":"p3-b06-035","paper":3,"topic":"Agricultural Pumping","kind":"fact","front":"What efficiency do agricultural pump sets typically reach, and why?","back":"30 to 55%. Efficiency varies because the water level in the intake changes, which pushes the pump away from its best efficiency point. Pumps are often oversized and locally made, chosen for low initial cost rather than efficiency.","explain":"Oversizing lets the pump draw water from declining depths and withstand large voltage fluctuations. Centrifugal pumps of 1 to 25 HP are most common, with higher ratings in some areas.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p223","order":3335},
{"id":"p3-b06-036","paper":3,"topic":"Energy Conservation in Pumps","kind":"fact","front":"Name five energy conservation opportunities in pumping systems.","back":"Ensure adequate NPSH at the site; provide basic instruments such as pressure gauges and flow meters; operate pumps near best efficiency point; modify the system to minimise throttling; adapt to wide load variation with variable speed drives or sequenced control of multiple units.","explain":"These are among the chapter's list. Other measures include avoiding free-fall gravity returns, repairing seals to stop dripping and fitting a booster pump for a few high-head areas.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p224","order":3336},
{"id":"p3-b06-037","paper":3,"topic":"Affinity Laws","kind":"fact","front":"If pump speed doubles, by how much does power go up?","back":"Eight times, because power is proportional to the cube of speed (2³ = 8).","explain":"Flow doubles and head quadruples at the same time. Small speed increases therefore sharply raise shaft power, shaft stress and bearing loads.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p226","order":3337},
{"id":"p3-b06-038","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Water Circuit","kind":"problem","front":"In a cooling water circuit, pipe A (100 mm, 1.5 m/s), pipe B (100 mm, 1.8 m/s) and pipe C (200 mm, 2.0 m/s) feed three heat exchangers. Total head is 34 m discharge less 2 m suction. Motor power is 50.7 kW at 90% motor efficiency. Find the pump efficiency.","back":"About 61%.","explain":"Total flow is the sum of the three pipe flows. Hydraulic power is flow × head × 9.81, and pump efficiency is hydraulic power divided by shaft power. The chapter uses 22/7 for pi and treats 3.4 kg/cm² as about 34 m head.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p224","steps":[{"prompt":"Flow in pipe A?","answer":"(22/7) × 0.1²/4 × 1.5 = 0.011786 m³/s","explain":"Flow = area × velocity; area = pi × d²/4."},{"prompt":"Flows in pipes B and C?","answer":"B: 0.014143 m³/s; C: (22/7) × 0.2²/4 × 2.0 = 0.062857 m³/s","explain":"Use the same area × velocity relation with each pipe's diameter."},{"prompt":"Total flow?","answer":"0.011786 + 0.014143 + 0.062857 = 0.088786 m³/s","explain":"Parallel branches from one pump add their flows."},{"prompt":"Total head?","answer":"34 m − 2 m = 32 m","explain":"The chapter treats 3.4 kg/cm² as about 34 m head and subtracts the 2 m suction head."},{"prompt":"Hydraulic power?","answer":"0.088786 × 32 × 9.81 = 27.9 kW","explain":"Hydraulic power = Q × head × density × g, with density 1000 kg/m³."},{"prompt":"Pump efficiency?","answer":"27.9 / (50.7 × 0.9) = 27.9 / 45.6 = 61%","explain":"Shaft power is motor input × motor efficiency; pump efficiency is hydraulic power / shaft power."}],"order":3338},
{"id":"p3-b06-039","paper":3,"topic":"Pumping Quiz","kind":"problem","front":"The shaft power of a motor driving a pump is 30 kW. Motor efficiency is 0.9 and pump efficiency is 0.6. What power is transmitted to the water?","back":"16.2 kW (option a in the chapter's quiz).","explain":"The chapter gives no answer key. This reading applies both efficiencies in series: 30 kW × 0.9 = 27 kW at the pump shaft, then 27 kW × 0.6 = 16.2 kW to the water. If the 30 kW is taken as the pump shaft power, the answer would be 18 kW.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p226","steps":[{"prompt":"Power at the pump shaft?","answer":"30 × 0.9 = 27 kW","explain":"Motor efficiency applies to the power taken in by the motor."},{"prompt":"Power transmitted to the water?","answer":"27 × 0.6 = 16.2 kW","explain":"Pump efficiency is the ratio of hydraulic power to shaft power."},{"prompt":"Which option matches?","answer":"Option a, 16.2 kW (per the chapter's option list, given the reading above).","explain":"Option b (18.0 kW) would result only if the 30 kW were already the pump shaft power."}],"order":3339},
{"id":"p3-b06-040","paper":3,"topic":"Pump Speed Reduction","kind":"problem","front":"A pump delivers 35 m³/h at 1440 rpm with a discharge gauge of 4.4 kg/cm². The suction is 2 m below the pump centreline. Pump efficiency is 68% and motor efficiency 90%. If speed is halved, what are the new flow, head and motor power?","back":"Flow 17.5 m³/h, head 11.5 m, motor power about 0.9 kW (down from about 7.2 kW).","explain":"The gauge reading is taken as 44 m head, plus 2 m suction lift, giving 46 m. Flow scales with speed, head with speed squared and power with speed cubed. This assumes 1 kg/cm² is about 10 m of head.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p227","steps":[{"prompt":"Total head at the present duty?","answer":"4.4 kg/cm² ≈ 44 m; plus 2 m suction lift = 46 m","explain":"Head is the gauge pressure in metres of liquid plus the static suction lift."},{"prompt":"Hydraulic and motor input power at present duty?","answer":"Hydraulic = (35/3600) × 46 × 9.81 = 4.39 kW; shaft = 4.39 / 0.68 = 6.45 kW; motor = 6.45 / 0.90 = 7.2 kW","explain":"Hydraulic power divides by pump efficiency, then by motor efficiency, to give motor input."},{"prompt":"New flow at half speed?","answer":"35 × 0.5 = 17.5 m³/h","explain":"Flow is proportional to speed."},{"prompt":"New head at half speed?","answer":"46 × 0.5² = 11.5 m","explain":"Head is proportional to speed squared."},{"prompt":"New motor power at half speed?","answer":"7.2 × 0.5³ ≈ 0.9 kW","explain":"Power is proportional to speed cubed, so halving speed cuts power to one-eighth."}],"order":3340},
{"id":"p3-b06-041","paper":3,"topic":"Pump Speed Reduction","kind":"problem","front":"A pillar furnace cooling pump gives 60 m head and draws 13.4 kW, but the furnace needs 12.5 l/s at only 3.0 kg/cm². Estimate the new shaft power if the pump speed or impeller is adjusted to match the duty, and the saving.","back":"About 4.7 kW, a saving of about 8.7 kW (about 65%).","explain":"The required head is about 30 m, half the existing 60 m. Using the affinity relations, head scales with speed squared, so the speed ratio is about 0.71 and power falls with the cube, to about 35% of the original. This estimate assumes the 13.4 kW is at the current 60 m duty.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p227","steps":[{"prompt":"Required flow and head in SI units?","answer":"12.5 l/s = 45 m³/h; 3.0 kg/cm² ≈ 30 m","explain":"Convert the furnace requirement to the same units as the pump data."},{"prompt":"Head ratio and speed ratio?","answer":"30 / 60 = 0.5 head ratio, so speed (or diameter) ratio = √0.5 ≈ 0.71","explain":"Head is proportional to speed squared."},{"prompt":"New power?","answer":"13.4 × 0.71³ = 13.4 × 0.354 ≈ 4.7 kW","explain":"Power is proportional to speed cubed."},{"prompt":"Saving?","answer":"13.4 − 4.7 ≈ 8.7 kW, about 65%","explain":"Recommended measures are speed reduction (VFD) or impeller trimming. Check the answer against the pump manufacturer's curve."}],"order":3341},
{"id":"p3-b06-042","paper":3,"topic":"Pump Capacity Control","kind":"fact","front":"What are the pump capacity control methods covered in this chapter?","back":"Speed control (VFD, multiple-speed motor, impeller trimming or a smaller impeller), flow control valve (throttling), bypass control, stop/start control, and parallel pumps switched to meet demand.","explain":"Speed control is the most efficient method. Throttling and bypass waste energy, and stop/start needs storage in the system.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 06 p227","order":3342},
{"id":"p3-b07-001","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What are the two main categories of cooling tower?","back":"Natural draft and mechanical draft.","explain":"Natural draft towers use very large concrete chimneys to draw air through the media and are generally used for water flow above about 45,000 m³/hr. Mechanical draft towers use fans, are far more widely used, and are the focus of this chapter.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p231","order":3343},
{"id":"p3-b07-002","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What are the three airflow arrangements of mechanical draft towers?","back":"Counter flow induced draft, counter flow forced draft, and cross flow induced draft.","explain":"In counter flow induced draft, hot water enters at the top and air is introduced at the bottom and exits at the top. In cross flow towers, water passes over the fill while air enters from one side (single-flow) or from opposite sides (double-flow).","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p232","order":3344},
{"id":"p3-b07-003","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"Name the basic components of an evaporative cooling tower.","back":"Frame and casing, fill, cold water basin, drift eliminators, air inlet, louvers, nozzles and fans.","explain":"Fill maximises water-air contact to aid heat transfer. Drift eliminators capture droplets carried in the air stream, and nozzles wet the fill with water spray.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p232","order":3345},
{"id":"p3-b07-004","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What are the two types of fill, and which is more efficient?","back":"Splash fill and film fill. Film fill is more efficient.","explain":"Film fill gives the same heat transfer in a smaller volume than splash fill. Splash fill breaks water into droplets as it falls over horizontal splash bars, and plastic splash fill performs better than wood splash fill.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p232","order":3346},
{"id":"p3-b07-005","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What is the function of drift eliminators?","back":"They capture water droplets entrapped in the air stream that would otherwise be lost to the atmosphere.","explain":"Drift is water lost as droplets, separate from evaporation loss. The chapter gives typical drift specifications in its drift loss section.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p233","order":3347},
{"id":"p3-b07-006","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"Which fan types are used in induced draft and forced draft towers?","back":"Propeller (axial) fans are used in induced draft towers. Propeller and centrifugal fans are both found in forced draft towers.","explain":"Depending on size, propeller fans can be fixed or variable pitch. Fixed-pitch and variable-pitch fans differ in how they adjust airflow to load.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p234","order":3348},
{"id":"p3-b07-007","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"How does a fanless cooling tower move air without a fan?","back":"Pressure from the existing water circulation pump forces water through ejector nozzles at high velocity. The low pressure created sucks cold ambient air into the tower.","explain":"Ejector nozzles convert water pressure into kinetic energy of the air. The chapter states the minimum pressure required at the jet ejector nozzles is 0.5 bar.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p235","order":3349},
{"id":"p3-b07-008","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What are the energy and noise advantages of a fanless cooling tower?","back":"No fan or motor is needed, so fan power is saved (the pump needs only marginally more power). Noise from fans and motors is also eliminated.","explain":"Fan and motor vibration reinforces noise resonance in a conventional tower, and the fanless design removes that source. The chapter also notes very low maintenance when circulating water is kept clean.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p235","order":3350},
{"id":"p3-b07-009","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"Define cooling tower range.","back":"The difference between the cooling tower water inlet (hot) and outlet (cold) temperatures.","explain":"Range is set by the process heat load and the water circulation rate through the exchanger, not by the tower itself. Both range and approach should be monitored.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p236","order":3351},
{"id":"p3-b07-010","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"Define cooling tower approach. Which is the better indicator of tower performance, range or approach?","back":"Approach = cold water outlet temperature minus ambient wet bulb temperature. Approach is the better indicator.","explain":"Approach shows how close the tower brings the cold water to the ambient wet bulb temperature. Range is still monitored alongside it.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p236","order":3352},
{"id":"p3-b07-011","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What is cooling tower effectiveness, and what is its formula?","back":"Effectiveness (%) = Range / (Range + Approach) × 100.","explain":"Range + approach equals the ideal range, which is the cold water inlet temperature minus the ambient wet bulb temperature. Effectiveness therefore compares actual range with the ideal range.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p236","order":3353},
{"id":"p3-b07-012","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What is the empirical formula for evaporation loss in a cooling tower?","back":"Evaporation loss (m³/hr) = 0.00085 × 1.8 × circulation rate (m³/hr) × (T1 − T2), where T1 − T2 is the inlet-outlet water temperature difference.","explain":"The 1.8 factor is the evaporation (m³) per 10,00,000 kcal of heat rejected. The chapter cites Perry's Chemical Engineers' Handbook as the source of the relation.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p237","order":3354},
{"id":"p3-b07-013","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What are cycles of concentration (COC)?","back":"The ratio of dissolved solids in the circulating water to dissolved solids in the make-up water.","explain":"COC links directly to blow down. For the same evaporation, a higher COC means less blow down is needed.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p237","order":3355},
{"id":"p3-b07-014","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"How is cooling tower blow down calculated?","back":"Blow down = Evaporation loss / (COC − 1).","explain":"Blow down depends on the cycles of concentration and the evaporation loss. The chapter advises optimising the blow down flow rate to the site COC limit.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p237","order":3356},
{"id":"p3-b07-015","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What thermodynamic relation links the L/G ratio to the water temperatures and air enthalpy in a cooling tower?","back":"L(T1 − T2) = G(h2 − h1), so L/G = (h2 − h1) / (T1 − T2). L/G is in kg/kg.","explain":"Heat removed from the water must equal heat absorbed by the air. T1 and T2 are the hot and cold water temperatures (°C), and h1 and h2 are the air-vapour enthalpies at the inlet and exhaust wet bulb.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p237","order":3357},
{"id":"p3-b07-016","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"How does cooling tower size vary with heat load, range, approach and entering wet bulb, when the other three are held constant?","back":"Size varies directly with the heat load and inversely with the range, the approach and the entering wet bulb temperature.","explain":"A larger heat load needs a bigger tower. A wider range, a larger approach or a higher wet bulb each allow a smaller tower for the same duty.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p238","order":3358},
{"id":"p3-b07-017","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What is the coldest approach to design wet bulb that a manufacturer will typically guarantee?","back":"About 2.8°C.","explain":"The closer the approach, the larger and more expensive the tower. Initial selection is based on the average maximum wet bulb for the summer months at the site.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p238","order":3359},
{"id":"p3-b07-018","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"problem","front":"A tower cools 4540 m³/hr from 48.9°C to 32.2°C at a 26.7°C design wet bulb. Find the range, approach and effectiveness.","back":"Range 16.7°C, approach 5.5°C, effectiveness about 75.2%.","explain":"This is the chapter's own example of how the cold water temperature is fixed by the wet bulb plus approach. The same method applies to any tower duty.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p238","steps":[{"prompt":"Calculate the range.","answer":"Range = 48.9 − 32.2 = 16.7°C.","explain":"Range is the hot water inlet temperature minus the cold water outlet temperature."},{"prompt":"Calculate the approach.","answer":"Approach = 32.2 − 26.7 = 5.5°C.","explain":"Approach is the cold water outlet temperature minus the design wet bulb temperature."},{"prompt":"Calculate the effectiveness.","answer":"Effectiveness = 16.7 / (16.7 + 5.5) × 100 = 75.2%.","explain":"Effectiveness is range divided by the ideal range (range plus approach)."},{"prompt":"Comment on the approach.","answer":"A 5.5°C approach is wider than the 2.8°C closest approach manufacturers usually guarantee.","explain":"The chapter notes the closer the approach, the larger and costlier the tower, so a wider approach allows a smaller tower."}],"order":3360},
{"id":"p3-b07-019","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"Why is the ambient wet bulb, rather than the inlet wet bulb, normally specified for a tower?","back":"Recirculation of discharge vapour raises the inlet wet bulb, and the recirculation factor is not known in advance. The ambient wet bulb is therefore specified, and the supplier must cover the extra rise from its own equipment.","explain":"The ambient wet bulb is the temperature that generally exists in the tower area. Inlet wet bulb is the temperature of the air entering the tower and can be raised by recirculated discharge.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p239","order":3361},
{"id":"p3-b07-020","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"Can a cooling tower cool water down to the entering wet bulb temperature when a heat load is applied?","back":"No. Without a heat load the tower can cool water to the entering wet bulb in theory, but a thermal potential is needed to reject heat, so the approach is always above it.","explain":"The approach obtained depends on thermal conditions and the tower's capability. This is why a design wet bulb cannot be treated as a guaranteed cold water temperature.","flash":true,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p239","order":3362},
{"id":"p3-b07-021","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What is the effective heat exchange area of film fill compared with splash fill?","back":"Film fill about 150 m²/m³; splash fill about 30–45 m²/m³.","explain":"Film fill also allows a higher possible L/G ratio (1.5–2.0 against 1.1–1.5 for splash). Because it needs less air and pumping head, film fill gives a large power saving.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p241","order":3363},
{"id":"p3-b07-022","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"problem","front":"Air enters a tower at 26.67°C wet bulb carrying 24.17 kcal/kg and leaves at 37.8°C wet bulb carrying 39.67 kcal/kg. How much heat does each kg of air pick up?","back":"About 15.5 kcal/kg, compared with the 12.1 kcal/kg the chapter quotes for the cooler 21.1°C wet bulb case.","explain":"Air at a higher wet bulb can carry more heat, so the same tower rejects more heat per kg of air. This is one reason a higher design wet bulb gives a smaller tower.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p241","steps":[{"prompt":"Find the heat picked up per kg of air.","answer":"39.67 − 24.17 = 15.5 kcal/kg of air.","explain":"Heat gained by the air equals its enthalpy at exit minus its enthalpy at entry."},{"prompt":"Compare with the cooler case.","answer":"The chapter quotes 12.1 kcal/kg for air going from 21.1°C to 32.2°C wet bulb.","explain":"Higher entering wet bulb air picks up more heat per kg, so the warmer case rejects more per kg of air."}],"order":3364},
{"id":"p3-b07-023","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"Rank these tower designs from least to most power consumption: cross flow splash fill, counter flow film fill, counter flow splash fill.","back":"Counter flow film fill (least), then counter flow splash fill, then cross flow splash fill (most).","explain":"Counter flow heat exchange is more effective than cross flow. The chapter's comparison (Table 7.4) is for a 16,000 m³/hr water flow.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p242","order":3365},
{"id":"p3-b07-024","paper":3,"topic":"Cooling Tower","kind":"fact","front":"What are the main water-side problems of an open cooling system?","back":"Corrosion and/or scale formation, and biological or micro-biological fouling. Deposits from turbidity, sand, silt and corrosion products add to the problem.","explain":"Corrosion can lead to leaks in heat exchangers and to deposits of corrosion products. Algae can block tower nozzles and reduce the temperature drop across the tower.","flash":false,"source":"BEE Book 3 Ch 07 p243","order":3366}
]$cards$::jsonb);
